A||||3||Eric||What's going on here?
Z||000000||Eric||07-29-99||05:22 PM||||I tore down my engine last night and found a few odd things.  First of all, the rods were numbered.  They were not numbered in order like you would expect, but were, in fact, numbered up to 8, with a majority being #2.  Also the pistons were flat.  I always thought they were dished.  The head gasket and sealing goop blocked water passages.  Is this craziness a result of the motor being rebuilt without my knowledge by a previous owner?
Z||000001||batboy||07-29-99||06:45 PM||||Yes! That engine has been apart and put back together, I hesitate to say rebuilt under the circumstances that you mentioned. 1987 and newer Generation II V6's with aluminum heads used dished pistons, but the HO engines with cast iron heads used flat-top pistons. Sounds like somebody put a hodge-podge of rods into that motor, maybe even small block V8 rods. There is a way to fit Chevy V8 rods into the 60 degree V6's. You must put different bushings into the wrist pin holes and the big ends must be narrowed, that may account for a #8 on one rod. If this is going to be a high performance rebuild, I'd get a new set of matched pistons and V6 rods. Also take that crank to the machine shop and let them do the works to it, check for cracks, then straighten and grind (under-size) the journals on the crank if needed. Sounds like a mental midget worked on that motor once.
Z||000002||theogre||07-29-99||06:46 PM||||the rod numbers don't mean anything outside GM. The flat pistons are normal in most factory setups.
Z||000003||lowCG||07-29-99||11:55 PM||||Don't know about the pistons,but the casting numbers are for other reasons than actual engine assembly.The only 60DEG V6 I've taken apart was from an old Citation,think it had dished pistons though,would make sense to use flat tops to increase compression from the factory.I know GM used plenty of silicone on it's motors also.Unless you can find an oversize number on the pistons,or any numbers that don't run in line with the crank on the main bearings,or is it the other way?A name brand on the bearings should indicate a rebuild. <br>Maybe Greendano could shed some light on this one?
